    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Jarl is glossed as 'Nobly-Born.'",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The note says Rig gives his own name to Jarl and that this could recognize Jarl as a king.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The note says the stanza's reference to teaching magic through runes fits Othin better than Heimdall.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A lost passage is said to have described the beginning of Jarl's journey.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Erna is glossed as 'The Capable,' and the note defines a hersir as an early local chief and highest recognized authority before the establishment of a kingdom in Norway.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The sons' names of Jarl and Erna are glossed with kinship and inheritance terms, and Kon is glossed as 'Son (of noble birth).'",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The note explains that 'Konr ungr' could contract into 'konungr,' the regular word meaning king.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Runes are described as magic charms that can dull swordblades and quench flames.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Rig-Jarl is identified as Kon's father.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "A crow is treated as a mentor-type bird, and the note says birds often play this role in Norse literature.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "A cited Latin epitome says Rig married Dana, daughter of Danp, won royal title for his province, and left Dan as heir.",
